This directory holds a Java implementation of the webrtc::PeerConnection API, as
well as the JNI glue C++ code that lets the Java implementation reuse the C++
implementation of the same API.

To build the Java API and related tests, make sure you have a WebRTC checkout
with Android specific parts. This can be used for linux development as well by
configuring gn appropriately, as it is a superset of the webrtc checkout:
fetch --nohooks webrtc_android
gclient sync

You also must generate GN projects with:
--args='target_os="android" target_cpu="arm"'

More information on getting the code, compiling and running the AppRTCMobile
app can be found at:
https://webrtc.org/native-code/android/

To use the Java API, start by looking at the public interface of
org.webrtc.PeerConnection{,Factory} and the org.webrtc.PeerConnectionTest.

To understand the implementation of the API, see the native code in src/jni/pc/.

TURN TLS certificate verification
---------------------------------
When no application certificate verifier is installed, the Android platform
verifier can validate a chain that the built-in root certificates do not trust.
The TLS connection's hostname selects the application's Network Security
Configuration (NSC). Hostname matching is checked separately by SSLAdapter.
With built-in roots enabled, an accepted chain does not reach the platform
fallback, so NSC anchors and pins are not a global veto over the built-in roots.
An application-provided verifier replaces the platform fallback.

The hostname-aware native verifier preserves existing C++ overrides after a
rebuild, but changes the SSLCertificateVerifier vtable and an internal JNI
signature. Rebuild native consumers with the matching headers and update the
Java classes and native libraries together as one SDK/AAR. Mixing an old native
library with new Java classes, or the reverse, is not supported. The public Java
SSLCertificateVerifier callback is unchanged.

The platform_certificate_verifier_instrumentation_test_apk target exercises
Android's real RootTrustManager with its own NSC XML and test CA resource. It
checks the original hostname-free refusal, host-specific anchors and pins,
and missing-host refusal through the production Java entry point. Its test
certificates are valid from 2020 to 2120, and no device trust store is modified.
Run it on API 24 or later with:
  out/Android/bin/run_platform_certificate_verifier_instrumentation_test_apk
These tests do not exercise native hostname matching or a full TURN connection.
